 Please see our [Development guide](docs/development.md) for how to get Fizzy set up for local development.
 
 
+## Deploy
+
+A fork can run Fizzy on one Linux server with Docker. Edit `hostname` and `mail_server` in [`config/deploy.yml`](config/deploy.yml), fill in [`.kamal/secrets`](.kamal/secrets), then run `kamal setup` (from this repo, `bin/kamal setup`).
+
+Fizzy will be at `https://<hostname>` with TLS from kamal-proxy. SQLite and uploads live in the `fizzy_storage` volume, so they survive the next deploy. Background jobs run in the same container.
+
 ## Contributing
 
 We welcome contributions! Please read our [style guide](STYLE.md) before submitting code.
diff --git a/config/deploy.yml b/config/deploy.yml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..20228c8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/config/deploy.yml
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# One-box Kamal deploy for Fizzy.
+#
+# A Linux server with Docker. SQLite, the web process, and background jobs all
+# live in one container. kamal-proxy terminates TLS, so the app is served at
+# https://<hostname>.
+#
+# Before the first boot, fill in:
+#   1. hostname and mail_server below
+#   2. .kamal/secrets (SECRET_KEY_BASE, SMTP username and password)
+# then run:
+#   bin/kamal setup
+#
+# Uploads and the SQLite databases are on the fizzy_storage volume mounted at
+# /rails/storage, so they survive the next `bin/kamal deploy`. Jobs run in this
+# container via the Solid Queue Puma plugin (see config/puma.rb).
+
+<% hostname = "fizzy.example.com" %>
+<% mail_server = "smtp.example.com" %>
+
+service: fizzy
+
+# Local registry started by Kamal on the server. No Docker Hub account required.
+# To use an external registry instead, set image to your-user/fizzy and replace
+# this block with username / password: [KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD].
+image: fizzy
+
+servers:
+  web:
+    # SSH target. Use an IP here if port 22 is not reachable on the public hostname.
+    - "<%= hostname %>"
+
+# Let's Encrypt via kamal-proxy. The hostname must resolve to this server, and
+# ports 80 and 443 must be open. Do not terminate TLS in the app container.
+proxy:
+  ssl: true
+  host: "<%= hostname %>"
+  # Imports can be very large; 0 means no limit.
+  response_timeout: 3600
+  buffering:
+    max_request_body: 0
+    max_response_body: 0
+
+registry:
+  server: localhost:5555
+
+env:
+  clear:
+    RAILS_ENV: production
+    DATABASE_ADAPTER: sqlite
+    # Public hostname used for links, cookies, and host authorization.
+    APP_HOST: "<%= hostname %>"
+    APP_PROTOCOL: https
+    # Jobs share this container. Do not add a separate jobs host for a one-box deploy.
+    SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: "true"
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: "<%= mail_server %>"
+    SMTP_PORT: "587"
+    SMTP_DOMAIN: "<%= hostname %>"
+    SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO: "true"
+    MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS: "Fizzy <notifications@<%= hostname %>>"
+  secret:
+    - SECRET_KEY_BASE
+    - SMTP_USERNAME
+    - SMTP_PASSWORD
+
+# Named volume: persists across container replacement on the next deploy.
+volumes:
+  - fizzy_storage:/rails/storage
+
+asset_path: /rails/public/assets
+
+builder:
+  # Match the server. amd64 is the usual VPS; use arm64 for an ARM box.
+  arch: amd64
+
+aliases:
+  console: app exec --interactive --reuse "bin/rails console"
+  shell: app exec --interactive --reuse "bash"
+  logs: app logs -f
+
+# ssh:
+#   user: app
